{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-75163",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & -1 & -7 & -15 & -9 \\\\  &  & 3 & 12 & 9 \\\\  & -1 & -4 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x+3} & =-{x}^{2}-4x-3 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+4x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+3)({x}^{2}+4x+3).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+3)(x+1)(x+3)=-(x+1)(x+3)^{2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=-1$ and a double root at $x=-3.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0+1)(0+3)^{2} \\\\  & =-(1)(3)^{2} \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-9),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-1,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-3.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251970/assets/c084c0059cecb1fa33430bc3761a4e5fbe23a44f3db59d61b9f2acfc74a87fa7.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
